<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="section-1-1">
    <title>Section 1.1 Linear Equations and Rational Equations</title>

    <subsection xml:id="idea-1-linear-equations">
        <title>Linear Equations</title>
        <p>
            Linear equations are also known as equations of first degree. That is, variables have <em>no written exponents</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            In General, this would exclude equations with variables in the denominators of fractions, since they would have an 
            exponent of <m>-1</m>. However, we will see some equations with variables in their denominators that are <em>equivalent to</em> 
            linear equations.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="idea-2-solving-equations">
        <title>Solving Equations</title>
        <p>
            To solve an equation means to find all possible solutions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Good News! When solving an equation you can do anything you want—as long as you do it twice. Once to the whole left side and 
            once to the whole right side.
        </p>
        
        <algorithm number="no">
            <title>Problem-Solving Strategy</title>
            <p>The most common things we do:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Add/subtract</em> same value from both sides</li>
                <li><em>Multiply/divide</em> both sides by the same non-zero value</li>
            </ul>
            <p>We solve equations by:</p>
            <ol>
                <li><em>Distribute</em> to eliminate parentheses</li>
                <li><em>Combine</em> "Like Terms"</li>
                <li>Use <em>Addition/Subtraction</em> to "collect" variables on one side, constants on the other</li>
                <li>Use <em>Multiplication/Division</em> to isolate the variable</li>
            </ol>
        </algorithm>
    </subsection>

    <!-- Video 1: Solving Linear Equations -->
    <subsection xml:id="solving-linear-equations-video">
        <title>Solving Linear Equations</title>
        
        <p><url href="https://echo360.org/media/9d4f201e-b274-47f8-909f-24c4eb74d050/public">Solving Linear Equations Video</url></p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ACTIVITY 1: New problem added before the existing one -->
    <subsection xml:id="example-linear-equation">
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>Solve:</p>
                <me>4x - 5 = 2(2x - x)</me>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Step 1: Simplify inside the parentheses on the right side.</p>
                <me>4x - 5 = 2(x)</me>
                <me>4x - 5 = 2x</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Collect variables on one side.</p>
                <me>4x - 2x = 5</me>
                <me>2x = 5</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Isolate the variable.</p>
                <me>x = \frac{5}{2}</me>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = \frac{5}{2}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <!-- ACTIVITY 2: Original problem -->
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>Solve:</p>
                <me>x - 4(x - 5) = 2 - (2 - x)</me>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Step 1: Distribute on both sides</p>
                <me>x - 4x + 20 = 2 - 2 + x</me>
                <me>-3x + 20 = x</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Collect variables on one side</p>
                <me>-3x - x = -20</me>
                <me>-4x = -20</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Isolate the variable</p>
                <me>x = 5</me>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 5</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="clearing-fractions">
        <title>Clearing Fractions</title>
        
        <!-- Video 2: Clearing Fractions -->
        <p><url href="https://echo360.org/media/ad586bdc-3b3d-4da4-8d1e-832db422fccc/public">Clearing Fractions Video</url></p>
        
        <algorithm number="no">
            <title>Clearing Fractions</title>
            <p>To clear fractions from an equation:</p>
            <ol>
                <li><em>Find the Lowest Common Denominator</em> (LCD) of ALL the fractions. (If you can't find the lowest common denominator, just multiply all denominators together.)</li>
                <li><em>Multiply</em> every term (on BOTH sides) by the LCD</li>
                <li><em>Simplify</em> each term. There should be no fractions.</li>
                <li><em>Solve</em> the new equation using your algebra skills.</li>
            </ol>
        </algorithm>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="example-clearing-fractions">
        <title>Example</title>
        
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>Solve:</p>
                <me>\frac{2x}{3} - \frac{5x}{6} - 3 = \frac{x}{2} - 5</me>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>LCD = <m>6</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by 6</p>
                <me>6\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right) - 6\left(\frac{5x}{6}\right) - 6(3) = 6\left(\frac{x}{2}\right) - 6(5)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>4x - 5x - 18 = 3x - 30</me>
                <me>-x - 18 = 3x - 30</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Solve</p>
                <me>-x - 3x = -30 + 18</me>
                <me>-4x = -12</me>
                <me>x = 3</me>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 3</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="variables-in-denominator">
        <title>What happens if there is a variable in the denominator?</title>

        <!-- Example 1 - Variables in denominator -->
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve: 
                    <me>\frac{9}{4} - \frac{1}{2x} = \frac{4}{x}</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    LCD: <fillin /> 
                    Restricted Values for <m>x</m>: <fillin />
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>LCD = <m>4x</m></p>
                <p>Restricted Values: <m>x \neq 0</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by <m>4x</m></p>
                <me>4x\left(\frac{9}{4}\right) - 4x\left(\frac{1}{2x}\right) = 4x\left(\frac{4}{x}\right)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>9x - 2 = 16</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Solve</p>
                <me>9x = 18</me>
                <me>x = 2</me>
                
                <p>Since <m>2 \neq 0</m>, this is a valid solution.</p>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 2</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <!-- Example 2 - Variables in denominator -->
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve: 
                    <me>\frac{1}{x-1} + \frac{x+1}{x^2 + 2x - 3} = \frac{1}{x+3}</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    LCD: <fillin /> 
                    Restricted Values for <m>x</m>: <fillin />
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>First, factor <m>x^2 + 2x - 3 = (x-1)(x+3)</m></p>
                <p>LCD = <m>(x-1)(x+3)</m></p>
                <p>Restricted Values: <m>x \neq 1</m> and <m>x \neq -3</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by <m>(x-1)(x+3)</m></p>
                <me>(x-1)(x+3)\left(\frac{1}{x-1}\right) + (x-1)(x+3)\left(\frac{x+1}{(x-1)(x+3)}\right) = (x-1)(x+3)\left(\frac{1}{x+3}\right)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>(x+3) + (x+1) = (x-1)</me>
                <me>x + 3 + x + 1 = x - 1</me>
                <me>2x + 4 = x - 1</me>
                <me>2x - x = -1 - 4</me>
                <me>x = -5</me>
                
                <p>Since <m>-5 \neq 1</m> and <m>-5 \neq -3</m>, this is a valid solution.</p>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = -5</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <!-- NEW ACTIVITY 3: Rational equation added at the end -->
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve: 
                    <me>\frac{3}{x+2} - \frac{1}{x-1} = \frac{5}{x^2 + x - 2}</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    LCD: <fillin /> 
                    Restricted Values for <m>x</m>: <fillin />
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>First, factor <m>x^2 + x - 2 = (x+2)(x-1)</m></p>
                <p>LCD = <m>(x+2)(x-1)</m></p>
                <p>Restricted Values: <m>x \neq -2</m> and <m>x \neq 1</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by <m>(x+2)(x-1)</m></p>
                <me>(x+2)(x-1)\left(\frac{3}{x+2}\right) - (x+2)(x-1)\left(\frac{1}{x-1}\right) = (x+2)(x-1)\left(\frac{5}{(x+2)(x-1)}\right)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>3(x-1) - (x+2) = 5</me>
                <me>3x - 3 - x - 2 = 5</me>
                <me>2x - 5 = 5</me>
                <me>2x = 10</me>
                <me>x = 5</me>
                
                <p>Since <m>5 \neq -2</m> and <m>5 \neq 1</m>, this is a valid solution.</p>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 5</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

</section>